{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Germ cells that travel to other parts of the body during fetal development can grow into germ cell tumors, and those forming in the brain or spinal cord are called CNS germ cell tumors.","source_span":"Sometimes while the fetus is forming, germ cells travel to other parts of the body and grow into germ cell tumors."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"The most common places for CNS germ cell tumors to form are near the pineal gland and in the area of the brain that includes the pituitary gland and tissue just above it.","source_span":"The most common places for one or more central nervous system (CNS) germ cell tumors to form is near the pineal gland and in an area of the brain that includes the pituitary gland and the tissue just above it."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"There are two main types of childhood CNS germ cell tumors: germinomas and nongerminomas.","source_span":"Germinomas    Nongerminomas"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Germinomas are the most common type of CNS germ cell tumor and have a good prognosis.","source_span":"Germinomas are the most common type of CNS germ cell tumor and have a good prognosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Choriocarcinomas, a type of nongerminoma, make the hormone beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG).","source_span":"Choriocarcinomas make the hormone beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (-hCG)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Yolk sac tumors, a type of nongerminoma, make the hormone alpha-fetoprotein (AFP).","source_span":"Yolk sac tumors make the hormone alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Embryonal carcinomas, a type of nongerminoma, do not make hormones.","source_span":"Embryonal carcinomas do not make hormones."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"The cause of most childhood CNS germ cell tumors is not known.","source_span":"The cause of most childhood CNS germ cell tumors is not known."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Signs and symptoms of childhood CNS germ cell tumors include unusual thirst, frequent urination, early puberty, or vision changes.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of childhood CNS germ cell tumors include unusual thirst, frequent urination, early puberty, or vision changes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"There is no standard staging system for childhood CNS germ cell tumors.","source_span":"There is no standard staging system for childhood central nervous system (CNS) germ cell tumors."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","statement":"Childhood CNS germ cell tumors rarely spread outside of the brain and spinal cord.","source_span":"Childhood central nervous system (CNS) germ cell tumors rarely spread outside of the brain and spinal cord."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the two main types of childhood CNS germ cell tumors?","answer":"The two main types are germinomas and nongerminomas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Where do childhood CNS germ cell tumors most commonly form in the brain?","answer":"They most commonly form near the pineal gland and in the area of the brain that includes the pituitary gland and the tissue just above it."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which hormone do choriocarcinomas, a type of nongerminoma, produce?","answer":"Choriocarcinomas make the hormone beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which hormone do yolk sac tumors produce?","answer":"Yolk sac tumors make the hormone alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Is there a standard staging system for childhood CNS germ cell tumors?","answer":"No, there is no standard staging system for childhood CNS germ cell tumors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_7","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of childhood CNS germ cell tumors?","answer":"Common signs and symptoms include unusual thirst, frequent urination, early puberty, or vision changes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Childhood craniopharyngiomas are rare, benign tumors usually found near the pituitary gland and the hypothalamus.","source_span":"Childhood craniopharyngiomas are rare tumors usually found near the pituitary gland (a pea-sized organ at the bottom of the brain that controls other glands) and the hypothalamus"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"There are no known risk factors for childhood craniopharyngioma.","source_span":"There are no known risk factors for childhood craniopharyngioma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Craniopharyngiomas are rare in children younger than 2 years of age and are most often diagnosed in children aged 5 to 14 years.","source_span":"Craniopharyngiomas are rare in children younger than 2 years of age and are most often diagnosed in children aged 5 to 14 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Craniopharyngiomas are usually part solid mass and part fluid-filled cyst.","source_span":"Craniopharyngiomas are usually part solid mass and part fluid -filled cyst."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Craniopharyngiomas are benign and do not spread to other parts of the brain or to other parts of the body.","source_span":"They are benign (not cancer) and do not spread to other parts of the brain or to other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Signs of childhood craniopharyngioma include vision changes and slow growth.","source_span":"Signs of childhood craniopharyngioma include vision changes and slow growth."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Blood hormone studies for craniopharyngioma diagnosis may check for unusual levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) or adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), both made by the pituitary gland.","source_span":"the blood may be checked for unusual levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) or adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). TSH and ACTH are made by the pituitary gland in the brain."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Three types of biopsy procedures may be used to sample craniopharyngioma tissue: open biopsy, computer-guided needle biopsy, and transsphenoidal biopsy.","source_span":"Open biopsy: A hollow needle is inserted through a hole in the skull into the brain.    - Computer-guided needle biopsy... - Transsphenoidal biopsy: Instruments are inserted through the nose and sphenoid bone"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","statement":"Childhood craniopharyngiomas are diagnosed and may be removed in the same surgery.","source_span":"Childhood craniopharyngiomas are diagnosed and may be removed in the same surgery."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","fact_refs":[2],"question":"At what age are childhood craniopharyngiomas most often diagnosed?","answer":"They are most often diagnosed in children aged 5 to 14 years, and are rare in children younger than 2."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Where in the brain are childhood craniopharyngiomas usually located?","answer":"They are usually found near the pituitary gland and the hypothalamus."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the typical physical composition of a craniopharyngioma tumor?","answer":"Craniopharyngiomas are usually part solid mass and part fluid-filled cyst."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Are there any known risk factors for childhood craniopharyngioma?","answer":"No, there are no known risk factors for childhood craniopharyngioma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What two pituitary hormones might blood hormone studies check for when diagnosing craniopharyngioma?","answer":"Blood hormone studies may check for unusual levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) or adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_8","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What three biopsy procedures may be used to obtain a tissue sample for craniopharyngioma diagnosis?","answer":"The three biopsy procedures are open biopsy, computer-guided needle biopsy, and transsphenoidal biopsy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"Childhood ependymoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the brain and spinal cord.","source_span":"Childhood ependymoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissues of the brain and spinal cord."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"Ependymomas form from ependymal cells that line the ventricles and passageways in the brain and spinal cord, and these cells make cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).","source_span":"Ependymomas form from ependymal cells that line the ventricles and passageways in the brain and the spinal cord. Ependymal cells make cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"The WHO groups ependymal tumors into five main subtypes: Subependymoma (Grade I), Myxopapillary ependymoma (Grade I), Ependymoma (Grade II), RELA fusion-positive ependymoma (Grade II or III), and Anaplastic ependymoma (Grade III).","source_span":"The World Health Organization (WHO) groups ependymal tumors into five main subtypes:         - Subependymoma (WHO Grade I).    - Myxopapillary ependymoma (WHO Grade I).    - Ependymoma (WHO Grade II).    -  RELA fusionpositive ependymoma (WHO Grade II or Grade III with change in the RELA gene).    - Anaplastic ependymoma (WHO Grade III)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"Most ependymomas form in the fourth ventricle and affect the cerebellum and the brain stem.","source_span":"Most ependymomas form in the fourth ventricle and affect the cerebellum and the brain stem."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"The cause of most childhood brain tumors is unknown.","source_span":"The cause of most childhood brain tumors is unknown."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"Signs and symptoms of childhood ependymoma depend on the child's age and where the tumor has formed.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms depend on the following:         - The child's age.    - Where the tumor has formed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"A lumbar puncture collects cerebrospinal fluid that is checked for tumor cells, with higher than normal protein or lower than normal glucose potentially signaling a tumor.","source_span":"A higher than normal amount of protein or lower than normal amount of glucose may be a sign of a tumor."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","statement":"Childhood ependymoma is diagnosed and removed in surgery.","source_span":"Childhood ependymoma is diagnosed and removed in surgery."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What type of cells do ependymomas form from, and what substance do those cells normally produce?","answer":"Ependymomas form from ependymal cells that line the brain's ventricles and passageways; these cells normally make cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the five WHO subtypes of ependymal tumors?","answer":"The five subtypes are Subependymoma (Grade I), Myxopapillary ependymoma (Grade I), Ependymoma (Grade II), RELA fusion-positive ependymoma (Grade II or III), and Anaplastic ependymoma (Grade III)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Where do most childhood ependymomas form, and which brain structures do they typically affect?","answer":"Most form in the fourth ventricle and affect the cerebellum and the brain stem."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Is the cause of most childhood brain tumors known?","answer":"No, the cause of most childhood brain tumors is unknown."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What does a lumbar puncture check for when diagnosing childhood ependymoma?","answer":"It collects cerebrospinal fluid that is checked for tumor cells, and for higher-than-normal protein or lower-than-normal glucose levels, which can signal a tumor."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_9","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What two factors do the signs and symptoms of childhood ependymoma primarily depend on?","answer":"They depend on the child's age and where the tumor has formed."}
